@* A page's controls in a column beside its content, and a sheet over it on a phone.

   ONE ELEMENT, TWO SHELLS
   ==================================================================================
   The element carries the popover attribute. Above the fold width the stylesheet overrides the UA
   popover rules and it is an ordinary grid item; below, it is a bottom sheet opened from a
   floating button. The sections are the same sections in the same order either way, which is the
   point: a control someone learned to find in the rail is in the same place in the sheet.

   Not two copies hidden by width. Two would mean two of every select, two of every generated id,
   and two controls bound to one handler -- a pair that agrees right up until it does not.

   WHY A COLUMN
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A bar has room for a chip, so a filter arrives as "Missing only" with no indication of what it
   is one of, and the row of chips explaining the filters becomes a strip of its own. A column has
   room for the heading, so the control says what it sets and the reading it produces sits in the
   heading of the group that decides it.

   It is sized to its page rather than to a fixed set of controls -- the collection's rail has
   seven groups and the wishlist's has three -- which is the argument for this over a sidebar.

   The page still has to say in its own bar what the controls are set to. A count whose filters are
   out of sight is a lie, and on a phone they are all out of sight. *@

<div class="page-rail sheet" popover id="@Id">
    @* Sheet only, by the stylesheet: on a column there is nothing to dismiss. *@
    <div class="rail-handle">
        <button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" popovertarget="@Id"
                popovertargetaction="hide">Done</button>
    </div>

    @ChildContent
</div>

@* The phone's way in. Two buttons at most, bottom-right, clear of the tab bar -- a bar here would
   be the third one, and the rule is two.

   Anything the page wants permanently on screen goes in Fabs, before the opener: the card grid
   puts its add/remove mode there, because it is the only control in the app that can destroy
   something and a destructive mode you have to open a sheet to check is a mode you will be wrong
   about. *@
<div class="rail-fabs">
    @Fabs

    <button class="rail-fab filters" popovertarget="@Id"
            aria-label="@(Count == 1 ? $"{OpenLabel}, 1 applied" : $"{OpenLabel}, {Count} applied")">
        @OpenLabel
        @* The count is what says whether opening it is worth doing. What the controls are actually
           SET to is words in the page bar -- a badge reading "2" reports that there are settings
           without reporting what they are, which is not the same thing. *@
        @if (Count > 0)
        {
            <span class="n">@Count</span>
        }
    </button>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// DOM id, which the opening buttons point at. Unique per page: two popovers sharing one id
    /// means popovertarget resolves to the first and the second can never be opened.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Id { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>What the floating button says — "Filters", "Tuning".</summary>
    [Parameter] public string OpenLabel { get; set; } = "Filters";

    /// <summary>
    /// How many of this page's controls are currently doing something, for the button's badge.
    ///
    /// Supplied rather than counted here, because the rail does not know what its groups mean. The
    /// page owns the settings, so the page owns the number — and it should be the same number the
    /// page's own subtitle spells out in words, so the two cannot disagree.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Buttons that stay on screen on a phone rather than folding into the sheet.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Fabs { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The groups themselves, in the order both shells present them.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
